[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Add a button** to the page (you can also use an existing UI element).  
2. **Listen for the click** and call `map.flyTo()` (or `map.easeTo()` if you want a little more control over the easing curve).  
3. **Pass a set of camera options** (center, zoom, bearing, pitch, duration, easing) that give a smooth, “flight-like” transition that works on any basemap – vector or satellite imagery.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – smooth fly on button click</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    /* Simple button styling – keep it accessible */
    #flyBtn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border: 1px solid #777;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    }
    #flyBtn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" aria-label="Fly to San Francisco">Fly to SF</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (replace the style URL with yours)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // light vector basemap
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // start over San Francisco
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0,
    // optional: improve accessibility for keyboard users
    hash: true               // URL reflects view (good for sharing)
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Helper: a smooth “fly” function
  // -------------------------------------------------
  /**
   * Fly the camera to a target location.
   *
   * @param {Object} opts
   *   @property {Array<number>} center   [lng, lat] in EPSG:4326
   *   @property {number}        zoom     Target zoom level
   *   @property {number}        bearing  Target bearing (° clockwise from north)
   *   @property {number}        pitch    Target pitch (° from horizontal)
   *   @property {number}        duration Transition length in ms (default 2000)
   *   @property {function}      easing   Optional custom easing (0-1 → 0-1)
   */
  function smoothFly(opts) {
    // Default values – you can tweak them per use-case
    const {
      center,
      zoom = map.getZoom(),
      bearing = map.getBearing(),
      pitch = map.getPitch(),
      duration = 2000,
      // A “cubic-out” easing feels like a real aircraft: fast start, gentle stop.
      easing = t => 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3)
    } = opts;

    // `flyTo` automatically calculates a curved path that respects
    // the globe-like projection. If you need a straight-line pan,
    // use `easeTo` instead.
    map.flyTo({
      center,
      zoom,
      bearing,
      pitch,
      duration,
      easing,
      // Optional: keep the map interactive during the animation
      // (default is true). Set to false if you want to block clicks.
      // essential for accessibility: users can still tab away.
      // interactive: true,
    });
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Wire the button
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    smoothFly({
      center: [-122.4194, 37.7749], // San Francisco City Hall (example)
      zoom: 14,
      bearing: 30,   // give a slight rotation for visual interest
      pitch: 45,     // tilt the view so the 3-D effect is visible
      duration: 2500 // a little longer for a more relaxed feel
    });
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  (Optional) Keyboard shortcut – improves accessibility
  // -------------------------------------------------
  document.addEventListener('keydown', e => {
    // Press “F” to trigger the same flight
    if (e.key === 'f' || e.key === 'F') {
      btn.click(); // reuse the same handler
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## Why `flyTo` (and not just `jumpTo`)?

| Method | What it does | When to use |
|--------|--------------|-------------|
| `map.jumpTo()` | Instantly sets the camera – no animation. | For initial view or when you need an immediate change (e.g., resetting after an error). |
| `map.easeTo()` | Linear-ish interpolation of the camera parameters. | When you want a **straight-line pan/zoom** (e.g., sliding a panel that moves the map horizontally). |
| `map.flyTo()` | Calculates a **curved, globe-aware trajectory** that mimics an aircraft “fly-over”. It also automatically adjusts the zoom so the camera never goes too far out or in. | **Most UI-driven transitions** – button clicks, menu selections, “go to my location”, etc. |

`flyTo` is the default choice for a smooth, pleasant experience.

---

## Fine-tuning the animation

### 1. Duration & easing
* **Duration** (`ms`) controls how long the animation lasts.  
  *Short* (≈ 800 ms) feels snappy; *long* (≥ 2500 ms) feels cinematic.  
* **Easing** is a function that maps the linear progress `t` (0 → 1) to a non-linear progress.  
  The cubic-out easing shown above (`t => 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3)`) is a good all-round default.  
  If you want a “bounce” feel you could use `t => Math.sin(t * Math.PI * 0.5)`.  

```js
// Example of a custom easing that starts slow, speeds up, then slows again
const easeInOut = t => t < 0.5
  ? 4 * t * t * t          // ease-in cubic
  : 1 - Math.pow(-2 * t + 2, 3) / 2; // ease-out cubic
```

### 2. Bearing & pitch
* **Bearing** rotates the map clockwise from true north. Small values (0-45°) give a subtle “look-around” effect without disorienting the user.  
* **Pitch** tilts the view. Values up to **60°** are safe; beyond that the perspective can become distorted on low-resolution devices.  
* When you change pitch, consider adding a **hillshade** or **terrain** source so the 3-D effect is meaningful (see the `maplibre-terrain-rendering` skill).

### 3. Keeping labels readable during the flight
If you are flying over a **satellite/aerial imagery** basemap, remember the cartographic rule from the `maplibre-cartography` skill:

* Use **white text** (`#ffffff`) with a **dark semi-transparent halo** (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`).  
* Set `text-halo-width` to `1.2`–`1.5` px for body labels.  

If you are injecting your own data layers, **add them before the first symbol layer** (see the “injection pattern” in the skill) so they never cover the basemap’s own labels while the camera is moving.

```js
// Example: insert a custom polygon layer under the basemap labels
map.addSource('my-polygon', { type: 'geojson', data: myGeojson });
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-polygon-fill',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'my-polygon',
  paint: { 'fill-color': '#c8f5cc', 'fill-opacity': 0.6 }
}, firstSymbolLayerId); // <-- second argument forces insertion before labels
```

---

## Accessibility checklist for the button-driven flight

| Concern | How to address it |
|---------|-------------------|
| **Keyboard operability** | The button is a native `<button>` element (focusable, `Enter`/`Space` activates). Add a keyboard shortcut (`F`) as shown. |
| **Screen-reader description** | `aria-label="Fly to San Francisco"` tells assistive tech what the button does. |
| **Contrast** | The button’s background (`rgba(255,255,255,0.9)`) against the map meets WCAG AA for most basemaps; you can test with a contrast checker. |
| **Motion sensitivity** | Some users prefer reduced motion. Respect the `prefers-reduced-motion` media query: if it’s set, skip the animation and use `jumpTo`. |
```js
if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
  map.jumpTo({ center, zoom, bearing, pitch });
} else {
  smoothFly({ center, zoom, bearing, pitch });
}
```
| **Focus management** | After the flight you may want to move focus to a newly-revealed UI element (e.g., a sidebar with details about the destination). Use `element.focus()` in the `flyTo` callback (`map.once('moveend', ...)`). |

---

## TL;DR – Minimal snippet

If you just need the bare-bones version:

```js
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  map.flyTo({
    center: [-122.4194, 37.7749],
    zoom: 14,
    bearing: 30,
    pitch: 45,
    duration: 2500,
    easing: t => 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3)   // cubic-out
  });
});
```

Add the accessibility and style-layer-ordering tips from the sections above, and you’ll have a **smooth, readable, and inclusive** camera transition that works on any MapLibre basemap—vector or satellite imagery. Happy mapping!
